    feat(blocks): a block beside a held sibling levels down to it (b12a2c-squeeze fix round 4 #1, item 4: B12 X4)
    
    X4 starts the sibling frames of a band level by moving the blocks that
    start later up to the earliest. A block entered past its first row from
    upstream is held there (the gap that link crosses was sized for its
    turns and label), and its siblings then stayed where they were: two
    frames of one band started apart and read as interleaved
    (`sib_stagger`), as a short standby site with a three-line title beside
    a staging block that a long link enters on its second row.
    
    `layout::level_sibling_frames` now levels the other way where a later
    block is held: the blocks that start before it move downstream to it,
    each whole, when none of them is in a fan above, none has a link
    leaving it downstream (the gap below, which the move takes from, was
    sized for those links), each keeps `LEVEL_DOWN_CLEAR` (two frame pads)
    from whatever lies below it across its breadth, and no line runs
    between it and the held block in a gap narrower than
    `LEVEL_DOWN_CORRIDOR` (eight clear gaps). Without the last rule, two
    lines in a narrow gap between the frames moved to the moved frame's
    side and a chip beside them lost every clean spot on its line.
    
    No repository fixture and no direction copy has the case: the three
    checks against the package start and against f9bb447 say no worse on
    every cell, gate message and copy; chips more than 3 px off their lines
    and the verifier's generated sets are unchanged; the default build is
    byte-identical (25 corpus SVGs).
    
    Test: `a_block_beside_a_held_sibling_levels_down_to_it` (four
    directions; `sib_stagger` 1 drawn TD, LR and RL before). No snapshot or
    gate changed.
    
